I haven't spent much time pondering the philosophy of the matter, but I
think the "boundary" and the difference between your hypothetical
Ludios-sacker's savescummed game and a paranoid Wizmode tester's game
is... opening a new game in Wizmode, wishing up the items you need,
gating to Ludios or whatnot.. is using builtin features of the game to
do your test.  Savescumming your character is using the operating
system the game lives on to sneak around the game's back and do things
it wasn't programmed to do. It's not about what's easier or harder or
"equivalent", it's about limiting yourself the game's features or
adding your own "feature" to a game to make something easier.

To mildly explore the philosophy behind it... if you're unsure of
whether to use a polearm or melee weapons at ludios, why not just take
both, try the one, and if it doesn't work say "oh crap, let me try
something else" and if that doesn't work, say "plan C, I better run!"
or even "plan C, I am dead so I have to start a new game."  That's the
way the game is designed to play.

Yesterday I had my very first ever run-in with Demogorgon.  I tried
zapping him with a wand of death.  Didn't work, and it cost me a
precious in-game /WoDeath charge to find out. (although I could've
studied a spoiler or Wiztested it to find the same thing).  So I tried
fighting him in melee.  I was surviving pretty soundly, but so was D
(and Asmodeus, and the Demilich that started the whole mess) and I
wasn't really getting anywhere.  So I q'd a precious in-game cursed
gain-level to get out of immediate danger and hoofed it back to my
altar/stash to regroup and come back prepared.  And it was fun!  I
could have saved there and Wiztested a couple of different
possibilities until I had something I was sure of, then gone ahead with
it, but that just isn't how the game is designed to work (and it
doesn't sound like much fun).



